          <div class="section-label">Complete Buyer's Guide</div>
          <h2 id="guide-heading">Everything You Need to Know About Buying a Home in Manhattan Beach CA 90266 &amp; 90267</h2>

          <p>Manhattan Beach is one of the most consistently coveted residential addresses in the United States. With a 2025 median home price of $3.3 million — a 10% increase over the prior year — and a PropertyShark ranking of #27 among the most expensive zip codes in the nation, 90266 sits in rarified company alongside zip codes in Beverly Hills, Newport Beach, and Northern California's most exclusive enclaves. Buying here is not merely a real estate transaction — it is an entry into one of Southern California's most prestigious and community-driven cities, and it requires preparation, financial sophistication, and a mortgage advisor who genuinely understands what jumbo lending at this price level requires.</p>

          <h3 id="why-mb">Why Manhattan Beach? Why Now?</h3>
          <p>Manhattan Beach has a combination of attributes that no other community in the South Bay — or arguably in greater Los Angeles — fully replicates. Two miles of pristine beachfront. A world-class public school system anchored by Mira Costa High School, consistently ranked in the top 1-2% of public high schools nationally. A walkable downtown centered on Manhattan Beach Boulevard that delivers independent dining, boutique retail, and community events at a quality level that exceeds what most cities ten times its size can offer. A population of approximately 34,000 that skews toward accomplished professionals, entrepreneurs, athletes, and creative executives who have chosen to invest in the city's community character as much as in their real estate.</p>
          <p>Manhattan Beach is also a market that rewards long-term commitment. The 10% price appreciation in 2025 follows a period that saw a 33% run-up between 2020 and 2022, a modest correction in 2023, and a recovery that has pushed the 2025 median back above the prior peak. Homes here have demonstrated the ability to preserve and grow value through multiple economic cycles — a function of the permanent scarcity of beachfront real estate and the stability of demand from a buyer pool that is not dependent on a single employer or industry.</p>
          <p>The question is not whether Manhattan Beach is a good investment. It is what it takes to actually buy here — and whether your financing is in place before you find the home you want.</p>

          <h3 id="six-neighborhoods">The Six Neighborhoods of Manhattan Beach</h3>
          <p>Manhattan Beach is small — just 3.88 square miles — but it contains six residential neighborhoods that differ dramatically in character, price, and buyer profile. Understanding these distinctions is the foundation of a successful search.</p>
          <p><strong>The Sand Section</strong> is the beachfront neighborhood west of Highland Avenue, home to The Strand and the Manhattan Beach Pier. This is the most globally recognized address in the city and among the most expensive coastal real estate in the United States. Strand properties regularly trade between $10 million and $25 million. Off-Strand Sand Section homes and walk-street properties typically range from $3 million for a dated original to $8 million or more for a contemporary build.</p>
          <p><strong>The Tree Section</strong> occupies the central residential core between the Sand Section and Highland Avenue, defined by its canopy of mature trees and the Manhattan Beach Greenbelt. This is where many long-term Manhattan Beach residents and repeat buyers ultimately settle — close to the beach and Downtown MB, genuinely walkable, and imbued with the neighborhood character that makes Manhattan Beach feel different from other wealthy coastal communities. Homes here typically range from $2.5 million to $5 million.</p>
          <p><strong>The Hill Section</strong> sits on elevated terrain above the Sand Section, with many homes offering panoramic ocean views. Custom-built on larger lots, the Hill Section delivers the views of coastal living without the beachfront price of The Strand. Prices range from $3 million to well above $8 million for significant view properties.</p>
          <p><strong>East Manhattan Beach</strong> covers all areas east of Sepulveda Boulevard, encompassing the Mira Costa District, Manhattan Heights/Liberty Village, and Manhattan Village. This is the most family-oriented section of the city, home to Mira Costa High School and Manhattan Beach Middle School. The Mira Costa District's 7,500 square foot lots are among the largest in the city. Price points here range from $1.5 million for original homes in Liberty Village to $4 million for large, fully renovated Mira Costa properties.</p>
          <p><strong>Manhattan Village</strong> is the city's only gated community, with 24-hour security, community pools, and HOA-maintained common areas. It attracts buyers who want the Manhattan Beach school district and lifestyle with the added benefits of a controlled, amenity-rich residential environment.</p>
          <p><strong>The 90267 zip code</strong> covers Downtown Manhattan Beach and the immediate Pier district — the most walkable, most urban, most beach-proximate blocks in the entire city. Properties here are typically higher-density than the surrounding residential sections but deliver an address that is at the absolute center of Manhattan Beach life.</p>

          <h3 id="financing-mb">Financing in Manhattan Beach — The Jumbo Reality at Scale</h3>
          <p>Every meaningful purchase in Manhattan Beach requires jumbo financing. With a median price of $3.3 million and an average sale price in 90266 approaching $4 million, the conforming loan limit of $1,209,750 is barely relevant as a discussion point — the vast majority of Manhattan Beach transactions involve loan amounts of $2 million or more, and transactions above $5 million are routine.</p>
          <p>At this level, the jumbo financing conversation is more nuanced than it is in markets like Torrance or even Redondo Beach. Here is what Manhattan Beach buyers need to understand about financing at this price point:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Super jumbo programs:</strong> Loans above $3 million or $5 million fall into "super jumbo" territory with their own underwriting standards, lender pools, and rate considerations. I maintain relationships with lenders who specifically originate at these levels for South Bay properties.</li>
            <li><strong>Asset-based lending:</strong> Many Manhattan Beach buyers are high-net-worth individuals with significant liquid and investment assets but complex income structures — stock compensation, partnership distributions, carried interest, real estate income. Asset depletion and asset-based lending programs can qualify buyers based on the value of verifiable assets rather than traditional income documentation.</li>
            <li><strong>Down payment at scale:</strong> A 20% down payment on a $4 million purchase means $800,000 in down payment alone, plus $90,000 to $120,000 in closing costs. Total cash to close can approach or exceed $1 million. Some super jumbo programs allow 10% down with compensating factors, which changes the cash requirement significantly but affects rate and PMI.</li>
            <li><strong>Reserve requirements:</strong> Super jumbo lenders typically want 18 to 24 months of PITI in verifiable liquid assets post-closing. On a $3 million+ loan, monthly PITI can approach $20,000 — meaning reserve requirements of $360,000 to $480,000 or more.</li>
            <li><strong>Appraisal complexity:</strong> Appraising properties in the $3 million to $10 million range in Manhattan Beach requires experienced local appraisers with access to comparable sales data. I work with appraisers who know the market and can support the value in high-profile transactions.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 id="self-employed-mb">Self-Employed and Complex Income Buyers in Manhattan Beach</h3>
          <p>Manhattan Beach has one of the highest concentrations of self-employed professionals, business owners, and executives with complex compensation structures of any residential community in Southern California. Entrepreneurs, entertainment industry executives, technology founders, professional athletes, and real estate investors are all well-represented in the buyer population — and all face the same challenge: their tax returns, after legitimate deductions, often significantly understate the income that actually supports their lifestyle and purchasing power.</p>
          <p>The solution is alternative documentation lending. Bank statement programs using 12 or 24 months of business or personal deposits are available in Manhattan Beach at the jumbo level, allowing buyers to establish qualifying income based on actual cash flow rather than AGI. For buyers with substantial investment portfolios, asset depletion programs can convert liquid assets into qualifying income at a standardized rate. For buyers with multiple income streams — base salary, equity compensation, rental income, partnership distributions — I specialize in assembling the complete income picture in a way that maximizes qualifying loan amount.</p>

          <h3 id="schools-mb">Mira Costa High School and Manhattan Beach Unified</h3>
          <p>Manhattan Beach Unified School District is one of the most significant drivers of property values in the city, and Mira Costa High School is its crown jewel. Consistently ranked in the top 1-2% of public high schools nationally, Mira Costa feeds directly into the nation's most selective universities and offers a depth of AP, honors, and extracurricular programming that rivals many private school offerings. For families with school-age children, the ability to access a public school of this quality — at no tuition cost — represents one of the most tangible financial benefits of owning in the Manhattan Beach school district.</p>
          <p>Elementary schools in the district — Grand View, Pacific, Meadows, and Pennekamp — are similarly well-regarded, and Manhattan Beach Middle School serves grades 6-8 with a strong academic and athletic program. For families considering the purchase through a school-district lens, understanding which elementary school feeds which neighborhood, and which part of the city offers the most direct path to Mira Costa, is an important part of the neighborhood selection conversation.</p>

          <h3 id="competing-mb">How to Win in the Manhattan Beach Market</h3>
          <p>Despite the high price points, Manhattan Beach is an active and competitive market. With an average of 34 days on market in the 90266 zip code and a market that saw 196 homes sold in a recent 12-month period, well-priced properties in desirable neighborhoods attract multiple qualified buyers quickly. Here is what positions buyers to compete and win:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A fully underwritten jumbo or super jumbo pre-approval</strong> — verified income, verified assets, verified credit. A pre-approval letter from a recognized South Bay lender carries weight with listing agents who know the market and know which lenders can actually perform at this level.</li>
            <li><strong>Proof of down payment and reserves</strong> — at the $3 to $5 million price level, sellers and their agents want to see evidence that the buyer has the capital to close. A pre-approval that references verified liquid assets reduces seller anxiety about the financing contingency.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed to close</strong> — I can close Manhattan Beach transactions in 21 days when a buyer is properly prepared. Sellers at this price level often have specific timing requirements, and a lender who commits to and delivers a 21-day close is a meaningful differentiator.</li>
            <li><strong>Understanding of neighborhood-specific financing nuances</strong> — walk-street properties, non-warrantable condominiums in 90267, PUD structures in Manhattan Village, and high-value view properties all have specific financing considerations. Knowing these in advance prevents surprises during escrow.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 id="next-steps-mb">Your Next Step</h3>
          <p>If Manhattan Beach is where you want to be — whether you are targeting The Strand, a Tree Section family home, a Hill Section view property, or an East Manhattan entry point at Mira Costa — the conversation starts with financing. I am Rudy Corona, a licensed California mortgage advisor with my office at 1611 S Pacific Coast Hwy #102 in Redondo Beach, minutes from Manhattan Beach. I specialize in the jumbo, super jumbo, and alternative documentation programs that Manhattan Beach buyers actually need, and I have the lender relationships to close your transaction in as few as 21 days.</p>
          <p>Call me at (310) 594-5362, email me at rudycoronalending@gmail.com, or fill out the form on this page. That conversation is free, it takes 20 minutes, and it will tell you exactly what it takes to buy in Manhattan Beach today.</p>
